1. itemgetter
What it does: itemgetter is a function that is used to retrieve an item from a list, tuple, or other indexable object (such as a dictionary or string) based on its index. It is especially useful when you need to sort or select specific items from complex objects like lists of tuples.

How it's used:

itemgetter creates a callable (function) that fetches the value at a specific index from a sequence or an iterable.
You can use it to sort lists of tuples or lists of lists based on a specific position/index of the items.

2. attrgetter
What it does: attrgetter is used to fetch attributes of an object. If you are working with custom objects (instances of a class), attrgetter can be used to get the value of an attribute of that object.

How it's used:

It takes the name of the attribute (as a string) and returns a function that gets the value of that attribute.
This is useful when sorting or filtering a collection of objects based on their attributes.

